\begin_layout Abstract
Teleoperation of manipulator robots allows operators in nuclear industry to perform dextrous manipulation inside constrained spaces such as a glovebox.
 Often times the glovebox contain many items and limited space for manipulation to be carried out and further having deterred external visibility and fatigue due to long hours makes it a risk and requirement for autonomous operation.
 Current methods for autonomous operation for rearrangement and constrained motion often fail due to brittle planning.
 In this paper,
 PriCT,
 a Reinforcement Learning based method is introduced which provides tractable plan for cluttered scenarios in constrained space.
 The method is evaluated with experiments and simulations in which the robot shows the replanning with minimal latency.
 Simulation show the robust nature of the plan.
 Experiments using a real robotic system show that the method can autonomously plan for any cluttered scenario.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Robots have provided safe operation in nuclear industry especially in gloveboxes that are contained environments for the safe handling of hazardous objects and materials,
 moving away from direct human involvement to usage of teleoperation techniques via haptic device systems such as RrOBO 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "rrobo_2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and modern COTS robotic solutions 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ozan2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 These methods have been used in constrained workspaces to perform cleaning,
 decommissioning and other manipulation tasks on legacy gloveboxes 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ozan2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
POCO 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "macpherson2016poco"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is the method of reducing the level of radioactive waste before decommissioning is performed.
 In gloveboxes the objective is to remove items out by rearranging the clutter and removing the prioritized objects first.
 This requires combined high level decision making in discrete space and motion verification in continuous space before a complete plan can be executed and problems such as these are solved using Task and motion planning.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The scenario deals with many objects that requires task planning to account for large scale decision making and further motion planning needs to avoid failures in confined spaces,
 as the pose decided by task plan are in task space coordinates and joint space control inputs are obtained using a kinematic control law,
 based on the robot differential kinematics 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "siciliano2009robotics"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An increase in safety and a reduction in mental load can be achieved through a robust task and motion planner,
 In recent years extensive work has been done on developing various methods for TAMP of which an in-depth survey was presented by Zhao 
\shape italic
et al.

\shape default
 and Caelan 
\shape italic
et al.
\shape default

\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Zhao_2025,garrett2021integrated"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In the literature it is seen that task planning is a bottleneck and doesn't scale well given a large amount of objects and further the non monotonous rearrangement instances in the clutter creates difficulty as this creates dependency constraints which requires some objects to move away and create space before an object can be placed,
 this is further subject to object size and shape that is an NP-hard problem 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "reif1994motion,ren2024multistage,wang2022efficient,wilfong1988motion"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this paper,
 we propose a Reinforcement learning based approach to TAMP that solves prehensile non-monotone object rearrangement planning problems in confined spaces with higher success rate and fewer steps and less distance travelled than any existing methods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Related Work
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Non monotonous rearrangement of obstacle is considered to be an NP hard problem and is often seen frequently in the field of Navigation Among Movable Obstacles (NAMO) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen1990practical,stilman2005navigation"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and Task and Motion Planning (TAMP) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "garrett2021integrated,srivastava2014combined"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Current method perform the planning on tabletop environments utilizing tree search with modified growing strategies or search hierarchy followed by backtracking 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "krontiris2016efficiently,havur2014geometric,gao2022fast,liu2022structformer,curtis2022long,goodwin2022semantically"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 For a cluttered and confined scenario it is much harder considering placement dependency created when multiple objects occupy the space of one,
 in these cases classical methods are limited in terms of scale of number of objects although providing optimal solutions for smaller pool they need to also factor in dependency and viability of motion plans.
 Smarter heuristics can be created unique to problem statements although a generic version will require understanding of larger context and also dependency factor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In recent years using Transformer architecture with reinforcement learning methods has provided with better understanding of large scale context 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "agarwal2023transformers"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Some methods have been developed for solving combinatorially expensive methods which can scale well,
 like the Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP).
 There have been implementations of introducing learning algorithms for Task and motion planning to qualitatively analyze long horizons.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A separate line of work removes the search altogether by learning a policy that outputs a solution directly.
 Kool et al.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kool2019attention"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 apply an attention-based encoder-decoder trained with REINFORCE and a rollout baseline to solve routing problems such as the Traveling Salesman Problem,
 producing near-optimal tours in a single forward pass at inference.
 POMO 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kwon2020pomo"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 improves on this by exploiting problem symmetry to sample multiple trajectories from different starting points per instance,
 giving a lower-variance baseline than the rollout baseline used previously and improving training stability.
 Luo et al.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "luo2023lehd"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 show that generalization to instance sizes far beyond training is governed less by encoder depth and more by decoder capacity,
 proposing a light-encoder heavy-decoder architecture that re-attends over the remaining candidate set at every decoding step,
 and report near-optimal solutions on routing instances an order of magnitude larger than seen during training.
 None of these methods,
 however,
 model geometric or kinematic feasibility,
 their action space assumes every candidate move is executable,
 which does not hold in a confined manipulation setting where a pick or place can fail due to grasp clearance,
 occupied targets,
 or ordering constraints.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Task and motion planning algorithms which are generic are PDDLStream and COAST that use symbolic planning and classical methods and further their extenstions are done using Learned methods.
 Ren et al.
 decouple non-monotone rearrangement in confined spaces into an object-stage topology solved with a multi-stage Monte Carlo Tree Search,
 reporting a 100% success rate on hard instances of seven to eight objects with real-world deployment,
 which represents the current object-count ceiling reported for this exact problem setting.
 Kulshrestha and Qureshi 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kulshrestha2023scl"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 use graph attention to capture structural dependency hierarchies in rearrangement,
 allowing generalization to scenes with an arbitrary number of objects,
 though their setting is tabletop rather than confined and does not encode a single-access-point geometry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The literature splits along one axis,
 methods that reason about geometric and motion-level feasibility do so through repeated search whose cost is either unbounded or re-incurred per instance,
 while methods that amortise inference into a trained policy do not model feasibility at all,
 treating every action as unconditionally valid.
 We are not aware of a method that combines an amortised,
 search-free task planner with a feasibility signal that is learned rather than discovered anew by search at every instance,
 which is the gap this paper addresses 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2026robotic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

