\begin_layout Section
Methodology
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section formally introduces the proposed RL based TAMP method and related modules in detail.
 The idea is to generate a symbolic task plan using the reinforcement learning algorithm which is a learned heuristic for inference time results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Task ordering as a Markov decision Process
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rearrangement problem is modeled as an MDP,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}\triangleq(S_{MDP},\mathcal{A}_{MDP},P,\mathcal{R})$
\end_inset

 such that a state is 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t_{\text{step}}}=((r,c)_{1,t_{\text{step}}},\dots,(r,c)_{i,t_{\text{step}}})$
\end_inset

,
 an action 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t_{\text{step}}}=((r,c)_{\text{occupied},t_{\text{step}}},(r,c)_{\text{clear},t_{\text{step}}}).$
\end_inset

 A reward function is 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{t_{\text{step}}}=\begin{cases}
1.0, & \text{if }(r,c)_{t_{\text{step}}}=(r,c)_{\text{goal},i}\in s_{T_{\text{goal}}},\\
-0.05 & \text{otherwise,}
\end{cases}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}(\tau)=\sum_{t_{\text{step}}=1}^{T}\rho_{t_{\text{step}}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The architecture is an encoder decoder model which generates the sequence based on the initial configuration.
 Training is done for 200,000 epochs over for grid sizes varying in the rage of 
\begin_inset Formula $3\times3-8\times8$
\end_inset

.
 The MDP is defined by a stochastic policy 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{\text{symbolic}}|s_{0},s_{T_{\text{goal}}}).$
\end_inset

 It is factorized and parametrized by 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{\text{symbolic}}|s_{0},s_{T})=\prod_{t=1}^{n}p_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\pi_{t_{\text{step}}}|\boldsymbol{s}_{0},s_{T},\boldsymbol{\pi}_{\text{symbolic\ensuremath{,1:t_{\text{step}}-1}}})$
\end_inset

 with gradient 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta|s_{0},s_{T})=-\mathbb{E}_{p_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{\text{symbolic}}|s_{0},s_{T})}[(\mathcal{R}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{\text{symbolic}})-b(s_{0},s_{T}))\nabla\text{log}p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\pi}_{\text{symbolic}}|s_{0},s_{T})].$
\end_inset

 it uses Kools method on TSP which works for this case as well.
 A good baseline 
\begin_inset Formula $b(s_{0},s_{T_{\text{goal}}})$
\end_inset

 reduces gradient variance and therefore increases learning speed.
 The baseline network receives no gradient updates and is the frozen copy of the policy evaluated on the same problem instances,
 its parameters are periodically hard-copied from 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 only when one-sided paired 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-test over a rolling window of evaluation such that the active policy's mean reward (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{\text{policy}}$
\end_inset

) is less than or equal to the baseline policy's mean reward (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{\text{baseline}}$
\end_inset

) weights are updated to match the current policy if this condition is statistically rejected,
 indicated by a calculated test statistic 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{\text{stat}}>0$
\end_inset

 and a 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\text{stat}}$
\end_inset

-value satisfying 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\text{stat}}<\alpha_{\text{stat}}=0.05$
\end_inset

.
 This statistical checkpoint prevents the baseline from chasing sampling noise during periods of stochastic training fluctuations,
 ensuring that each baseline promotion represents a genuine,
 reproducible strategy improvement.
\end_layout

